opening='The inner chamber is what lies past a house’s public gate and hall; Chan records bend that architectural depth into the inner matter of a teacher’s house, lineage formulations, or an encounter.'
body='An interlocutor asks whether two establishments differ in “public frontage and inner chamber,” making the outer/inner house contrast explicit. Wanan Daoyan says only one who enters the gateless gate can sit within the inner chamber. Sanfeng Hanyue Fazang calls Linji’s mysteries, essentials, guest, and host the inner matters differentiated within one phrase. Baiyu Jingsi says not to seek the inner chamber elsewhere; an unnamed monk says Zhaozhou’s walls admitted him and asks about the inner-chamber matter; Yingning Jing directs one who wants to enter deeply to inquire of an elder. The Zen bend preserves the spatial constraint—past frontage into the inside—while applying it to what is intimate or internal to a teaching house, without inventing a secret doctrine.'
